WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.430
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Build with Google Cloud

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.040
where we build
reference architectures.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.370
In this video, we will
build an e-commerce platform

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.510
on Google Cloud.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:10.800
What are your options?

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.660
Stay with me to find out.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.644
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.970
Let's say you're a retailer
that sells pet products, shoes,

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.350
cars, anything.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.080
Did you know that
digital commerce platform

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.540
can be built and modernized
in multiple ways?

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.390
I am Priyanka
Vergadia, developer

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.070
advocate here at Google Cloud.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.800
And today, let's see
what your options

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.040
are to build a digital
e-commerce platform on Google

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:44.610
Cloud.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:45.820
You ready?

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:46.350
Let's go.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.990
[MOTOR SPINNING]

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.630
First, you can build your
own e-commerce platform

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:53.980
on Google Cloud.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:55.820
Why does this make sense?

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.830
Because it provides the agility
to create new capabilities

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:03.520
using data insights and access
to machine learning and AI

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.440
tools within Google Cloud.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.500
There are two ways to
build your own platform.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.770
You could either migrate your
existing e-commerce platform

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:14.710
or modernize using containers.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:18.070
Migrate means lifting and
shifting your current platform

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.020
as-is into the Cloud.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:23.890
This is the lowest risk and
fastest way to get to Cloud.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:27.160
It is suitable for custom
built e-commerce stack

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:31.000
leveraging heavy OSS
or running on premises.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.240
And once you've migrated,
you are on your path

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.740
to modernization.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.740
You could choose to
migrate and improve

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.650
where you can upgrade
and improve or optimize

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:43.970
using containerised approach.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.540
This means you're willing
to move to containers

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.300
and are gradually moving
to Google Cloud managed

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.670
services, such as GKE.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:55.810
It sets the stage for a more
seamless modernization path

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.940
to microservices on GKE.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:01.060
If the goal is to modernize
your commerce application

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:05.680
platform completely for benefits
of Cloud and speed to market,

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.800
then you would re-architect
to adopt containers using

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.720
microservices architecture.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.480
This helps you shift
focus to creating

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.840
those differentiated customer
experiences for digital

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.760
and omnichannel revenue growth.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.400
We'll come back to architect,
the commerce migration

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.170
and modernization,
but before we do that,

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.870
let's also check out
the other options.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:30.010
The second option is, you can
create a headless commerce

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:31.750
platform on Google Cloud.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.990
This is where you actually
decouple the front end,

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:38.530
the mobile app, the CMS, et
cetera, from your back end

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.360
or your commerce layer.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.340
This approach
provides flexibility

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.830
to your marketing
teams to quickly alter

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:48.100
front-end elements to run
campaigns, promotions,

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.100
and create most advanced
customer experience

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.670
on your front end.

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:55.810
You're building front-end
experience on Google Cloud

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:57.850
and are integrating
with commerce

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:01.810
back ends from Google Cloud
marketplace offering partners.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:05.290
This unique approach offers
the benefits of the agility

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:09.010
to launch or change highly
personalized experiences,

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:12.070
flexibility to upgrade,
update, or replace

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.800
your dependent
systems, and scale

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.370
different parts of solutions
independent of each other.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:21.200
As you can imagine,
this approach

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.190
has a low total cost of
ownership, smaller IT teams,

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:28.490
and is not constrained
by a monolithic roadmap

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:32.270
and timelines to offer
new storefront features.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:33.230
Third option?

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:36.380
You could use out-of-the-box
SaaS solution on Google Cloud

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:40.910
marketplace, such as Shopify,
BigCommerce, or HCL Commerce.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:44.480
This will work for you if you
require minimum customizations

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.080
and can take advantage of
Google Cloud for customer 360

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.520
and data insights-driven
analytics.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.550
Which of these
approaches are you

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:56.210
leaning towards to build your
e-commerce platform, and why?

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:57.990
Share in the comments.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:00.710
Let's dive into the
first two approaches--

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:04.130
build your own and headless
commerce-- one by one

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:06.470
to understand what
it takes to build

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:10.150
and which one makes
sense for your use case.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.880
Now let's architect
the build your own,

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:15.500
migrate, and improve approach.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:18.519
This means you're not
just migrating but also

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:21.790
looking to break down a
legacy monolith commerce

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:25.750
application into a set of
microservices that can help you

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:29.440
gain agility in launching
those new features in a way

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:32.260
to improve customer
experience by leveraging

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:36.590
data insights that are powered
by AI and ML capabilities.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:38.300
In order to do
this, you're going

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:41.870
to need to migrate and
improve using containerization

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:44.390
on Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:46.430
Let's see how.

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:48.860
You would deploy your
application servers

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:53.300
and your database as services
and containers inside a GKE

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:54.440
cluster.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:57.650
You can accelerate this
process of moving to GKE

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:01.820
by using Migrate for Anthos,
Google's migration automation

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:03.570
tool.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:05.200
All your static content--

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.440
including the
images, media assets,

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.890
web files-- will be
stored in Cloud Storage.

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:15.090
Any other application systems
that still exist on premises

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:18.330
will be hooked up via a
dedicated interconnect Cloud

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.400
VPN or API management.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.660
Now let's see the request flow.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.090
The user makes a request
from their phone,

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:29.460
website, or chat
application, which

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:34.680
then gets resolved by cloud DNS
to a global any past IP address

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.020
for Cloud Load Balancing.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:40.080
Cloud Armor works
alongside Load Balancing

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:43.440
to provide DDoS protection
and web application

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.580
firewall that detects and
blocks layer 7 attacks.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.790
reCAPTCHA Enterprise
provides bot protection.

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:55.950
And API defense provides
protection for abuse of APIs.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:59.400
Combination of Cloud
Armor, Apigee API defense,

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:02.820
and reCAPTCHA Enterprise
offer the web application

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.450
and API Protection.

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:08.300
Once the request is
validated by these,

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.830
it is then sent
to the web server.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:13.490
The web server then grabs
the static content--

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:16.160
such as your images,
CSS, your JavaScript--

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:19.460
to render that page
from Cloud Storage.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:24.110
It also stores them in Cloud CBN
for next request, which helps

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:27.560
with performance and latency.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.280
Next, web server
would need to interact

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:33.520
with the app server for
business logic, such as,

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:35.530
is this item in-store?

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:39.670
The internal load balancing
between the web and application

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:43.120
servers help
distribute the load.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:45.670
Once the request lands
on the app server,

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:48.790
it interacts with the
database to get the response.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:51.790
Yes, the item is in-store,
and there are four more

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:54.490
left, or something like that.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:56.860
Gets this information
and then sends it back

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:59.980
to the user via web servers.

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:03.130
Now, if the request
requires the app server

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:07.370
to gather information from a
service that is on-premises,

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:10.780
then it can make a call
to the service via an API

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:13.810
through Apigee
API gateway or VPN

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:16.540
over the internet or
just direct connectivity

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:19.200
to that data center.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:22.860
If the request was a surge
or recommendations, then

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:26.610
app server interfaces with
the Google retail search

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:31.080
or the recommendations API to
present the appropriate results

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.340
to the user.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:36.970
Architecturally, build your
own, migrate, and improve

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:41.380
provides you the flexibility
to add features, ability

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:45.880
to scale easily by adding more
GKE clusters during traffic

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:46.510
spikes.

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:49.420
It also offers
applications the security

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:53.170
using a combination of
Cloud Armor, Load Balancer,

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:56.080
reCAPTCHA, and API defense.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:59.260
From the business perspective,
the build your own

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:03.250
and migrate and improve
approach enables the integration

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:05.530
with the data
platform which creates

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:09.790
a strong closed loop between the
data platform and the commerce

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:10.690
platform.

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:13.150
You can collect the
first party customer data

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:16.840
from shopper interactions on
their modern digital commerce

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:20.080
platform to use in
your data platform

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:23.950
for predictive analytics
and marketing activation.

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:27.520
You can also gain insights
from data in data platform

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:31.540
to offer personalized shopping
experiences and promotions

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:33.730
on the digital commerce site.

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:35.980
Are you ready to
migrate and improve?

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:38.169
We've got a link to
the documentation below

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:39.350
to help you out.

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:42.760
Now, how would you architect
your migration as-is

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.640
or lift and shift your
e-commerce platform

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:47.320
to Google Cloud?

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:50.500
Say your priority is
to move to Google Cloud

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:54.440
as quickly as possible or
you have a homegrown OSS

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:58.000
stack based on-premises,
an e-commerce platform that

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:02.260
is unique and works well and
you just want to migrate.

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:05.380
That means you just
want to migrate as-is

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:08.080
without making any changes.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.870
Just get to the Cloud so
you can take that first step

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:13.190
towards modernization.

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:15.300
For an as-is
migration like this,

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:17.810
we recommend migrating
to Compute Engine

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:19.310
virtual machines.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:21.890
Let's see how to architect this.

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:24.590
Your request flow pretty
much stays the same,

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:26.600
except for your
web and application

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:28.820
servers and your database.

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:31.400
You will create your web
and application servers

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:34.520
on Compute Engine managed
instance groups, which

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:37.880
will scale based on the number
of requests you receive.

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:41.510
You will also deploy your
primary and secondary databases

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:44.000
on Compute Engine instances.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.640
This architecture
offers the ability

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:51.440
to scale the web and app tiers
independently using the managed

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:55.190
instance groups and provides
low-latency responses back

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:58.490
to the end customer to keep
them engaged in their buying

00:09:58.490 --> 00:10:01.200
journey.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:03.240
You have the same
application security

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:06.540
using a combination of
Cloud Armor, Load Balancer,

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:09.090
reCAPTCHA, and API Defense.

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:12.540
In addition, it allows
you as a retailer

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:16.920
to quickly meet unexpected user
traffic spikes without having

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.260
a large-sized
deployment initially,

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:23.010
but automatically scale
easily up and down enabled

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:25.140
by your configuration policies.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:28.890
Are you ready to migrate your
e-commerce application as-is?

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:32.440
We've got a link in the
documentation to help you out.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:35.710
Now, let's say you have
migrated to Google Cloud using

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:38.730
GKE or Compute Engine,
or you're building

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:42.540
an entire modernized e-commerce
platform from scratch.

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:47.070
How would you go from here to
modernized, fully-containerized

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:48.900
microservices architecture?

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:49.800
Let's see.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:53.520
All your requests flow stays
the same as you saw earlier.

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:56.190
What changes is that
now you're going

00:10:56.190 --> 00:11:00.330
to think about smaller
services and separate them out

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:02.220
into four layers--

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:05.760
presentation layer, which could
be your single-page application

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:10.230
builder and content; a services
layer, which would include

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:14.490
services, such as sessions,
search, account, inventory,

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:16.620
orders, and anything
else that you can think

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:20.490
of; the storage layer,
which would be your storage

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:22.570
choices for those services.

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:24.210
There could be
different storages

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:26.400
based on the kind of service--

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:30.840
for example, Cloud SQL or
Spanner for inventory products

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:34.200
and maybe Firestore
for sessions.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.230
Cache layer would be
your transient cache

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:41.040
using memory store to
access recently queried data

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:44.620
without querying the
storage layer again.

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:48.490
You can now see the flexibility
that this architecture allows

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:49.480
you to have.

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:53.800
You can add and deploy
any new services any time

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:56.650
without affecting
any other services.

00:11:56.650 --> 00:11:58.510
You can also have
different teams

00:11:58.510 --> 00:12:01.570
working on different services
in different programming

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:03.310
languages.

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:06.340
Lots more flexibility both
from the development side

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:08.230
and from the
business side, which

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:12.510
leads to happy developers
and happy customers.

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:14.010
And you have enabled
your business

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:16.620
to have a strong closed
loop between the data

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:18.690
platform and the
commerce platform

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:22.440
to understand customer
behavior and offer personalized

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:24.720
experiences.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:27.150
Another huge advantage
of this approach

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:31.710
is the seamless integration with
other Google Cloud Services,

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:37.050
such as Workspace, Maps,
G Pay, mobile, Android,

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:39.430
Chrome devices, and more.

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:42.090
Are you ready to start your
e-commerce modernization

00:12:42.090 --> 00:12:42.780
journey?

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.930
We've got a link to a sample
code repository for you

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:47.250
to get started.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:51.720
Now, let's take a deep dive into
the headless commerce solution.

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:54.570
As a retailer, you might
want to own and control

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:57.570
the front end for providing
an engaging and differentiated

00:12:57.570 --> 00:13:01.110
shopping experience and leverage
the ready-to-use back-end

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:03.690
commerce building
blocks for capabilities,

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:07.590
such as product catalog,
pricing, promotions, cards,

00:13:07.590 --> 00:13:09.780
shipping, accounts,
and other things.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:14.430
In this fast-paced world
where user behavior changes

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:16.950
every day, headless
commerce approach

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:19.740
is increasingly preferred
for speed-to-market.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:24.060
You can easily launch new
experiences quickly and benefit

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:27.000
from innovations in
back-end capabilities

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:29.950
added by our partners on
Google Cloud Marketplace,

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:31.920
such as Commerce Tools.

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:33.510
How would you architect this?

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:34.380
Well, let's see.

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:38.040
The user would have the
same secured experience

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:41.940
using web application and
API security solution.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:45.720
The front end is deployed
on GKE or Cloud Run

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:49.110
and can use Cloud Storage
for static assets,

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:52.890
and Firestore or Cloud SQL
for your dynamic storage

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:54.960
and database options.

